src/pages/events/fed-rate/2026-09-16-unanimous-quarter-point-hike.astro:64:        <p data-layer="narrative">Financial markets put {decision.preDecisionHikeOdds} percent odds on a quarter-point increase the day before the decision. The new information was how completely the committee had moved: July's {decision.julyVote.forHold}–{decision.julyVote.forIncrease} hold became a {event.kpis[1].value} increase, and {projections.expectingAnotherIncrease} of {projections.participantCount} policymakers expected another increase by year-end.<Cite s="abc-fed-preview-2026-09-16" passage="odds of a quarter-point rate hike stood at 94%" /><Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-07-29" passage="approved the following statement for release by a 9 – 3 vote" /><Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="approved the following statement for release by a 12 – 0 vote" /><Cite s="reuters-fed-decision-2026-09-16" passage="16 of 18 policymakers anticipate at least one more quarter-percentage-point hike" /></p>
src/pages/events/fed-rate/2026-09-16-unanimous-quarter-point-hike.astro:91:        <p data-layer="narrative">Headline economic growth slowed to {growth.secondQuarterGdp} percent from {growth.firstQuarterGdp} percent. Beneath that slowdown, consumer spending and private fixed investment grew {growth.secondQuarterPrivateDomesticSales} percent, a measure of domestic demand.<Cite s="bea-gdp-q2-second-estimate-2026-08-26" passage="Real gross domestic product (GDP) increased at an annual rate of 1.5 percent" /><Cite s="bea-gdp-q2-second-estimate-2026-08-26" passage="the sum of consumer spending and gross private fixed investment, increased 4.2 percent" /></p>
src/pages/events/fed-rate/2026-09-16-unanimous-quarter-point-hike.astro:128:      <section id="projections">
src/pages/events/fed-rate/2026-09-16-unanimous-quarter-point-hike.astro:132:        <p data-layer="narrative">Sixteen of {projections.participantCount} policymakers expected at least one more quarter-point increase by the end of 2026. Their median year-end rate rose from {projections.june2026Median} percent in June to {projections.september2026Median} percent in September.<Cite s="reuters-fed-decision-2026-09-16" passage="16 of 18 policymakers anticipate at least one more quarter-percentage-point hike" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="Federal funds rate         4.1    4.1" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="June projection          3.8    3.6" /></p>
src/pages/events/fed-rate/2026-09-16-unanimous-quarter-point-hike.astro:190:          <li>What the September deliberations add. The minutes are due {decision.minutesDueLabel}, three weeks after the decision.<Cite s="fomc-calendar-2026-09-19" passage="The minutes of regularly scheduled meetings are released three weeks after the date of the policy decision." /></li>
src/pages/events/government-funding/2026-09-01-house-passes-stopgap.astro:19:import { event, houseVote, senateVote, calendar, hemp, julyVote, januaryVote, ruleVote, buddVote, timeline, votesRows, dateLine, exceptionRows } from "../../../data/governmentfunding202609.mjs";
src/pages/events/government-funding/2026-09-01-house-passes-stopgap.astro:178:        <p data-layer="narrative">The July bill Cole and Johnson credited was a different bill. On July 21 the House passed H.R. 9770, "Making continuing appropriations for fiscal year 2027, and for other purposes," {julyVote.yeas} to {julyVote.nays}, with a December 4 date; Republicans {julyVote.republican.yea} to {julyVote.republican.nay}, Democrats {julyVote.democratic.yea} to {julyVote.democratic.nay}. The Senate never voted on it. Cole told the House that the Senate "built on our plan"; what the Senate did was write its own text into the January trade bill, and the House adopted that text without change.<Cite s="house-clerk-roll272" passage="Making continuing appropriations for fiscal year 2027, and for other purposes" /><Cite s="cole-floor-2026-09-01" passage="in July the House proactively passed H.R. 9770" /></p>
src/pages/events/government-funding/2026-09-01-house-passes-stopgap.astro:194:          <p>House roll call 272 on July 21, 2026, is the July bill: "Making continuing appropriations for fiscal year 2027, and for other purposes," on passage, {julyVote.yeas} to {julyVote.nays}. Republicans voted {julyVote.republican.yea} to {julyVote.republican.nay}. Democrats voted {julyVote.democratic.yea} to {julyVote.democratic.nay}. Independent 1 to 0.<Cite s="house-clerk-roll272" passage="Making continuing appropriations for fiscal year 2027, and for other purposes" /></p></SourcedBlock>
