checks/audits/fed-rate--2026-09-16-unanimous-quarter-point-hike-2026-09-20-since-8bf7b3a6-entailment.log:5067:+        <p data-layer="narrative">Financial markets put {decision.preDecisionHikeOdds} percent odds on a quarter-point increase the day before the decision. The new information was how completely the committee had moved: July's {decision.julyVote.forHold}–{decision.julyVote.forIncrease} hold became a {event.kpis[1].value} increase, and {projections.expectingAnotherIncrease} of {projections.participantCount} policymakers expected another increase by year-end.<Cite s="abc-fed-preview-2026-09-16" passage="odds of a quarter-point rate hike stood at 94%" /><Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-07-29" passage="approved the following statement for release by a 9 – 3 vote" /><Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="approved the following statement for release by a 12 – 0 vote" /><Cite s="reuters-fed-decision-2026-09-16" passage="16 of 18 policymakers anticipate at least one more quarter-percentage-point hike" /></p>
checks/audits/fed-rate--2026-09-16-unanimous-quarter-point-hike-2026-09-20-since-8bf7b3a6-entailment.log:5092:-        <p data-layer="narrative">Seven weeks earlier, the committee had held the range at 3.50–3.75 percent by a {decision.julyVote.forHold}–{decision.julyVote.forIncrease} vote. The July minutes say most participants wanted more information before acting, while several already favored a quarter-point increase.<Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-07-29" passage="approved the following statement for release by a 9 – 3 vote" /><Cite s="fomc-minutes-2026-07-29" passage="most participants supported maintaining the current target range" /><Cite s="fomc-minutes-2026-07-29" passage="Several participants favored an increase of 25 basis points" /></p>
checks/audits/fed-rate--2026-09-16-unanimous-quarter-point-hike-2026-09-20-since-8bf7b3a6-entailment.log:5098:-        <p data-layer="narrative">Growth was not uniformly fast: real GDP increased at a {growth.secondQuarterGdp} percent annual rate in the second quarter, down from {growth.firstQuarterGdp} percent in the first. Private domestic final sales, consumer spending plus private fixed investment, grew {growth.secondQuarterPrivateDomesticSales} percent in the same quarter.<Cite s="bea-gdp-q2-second-estimate-2026-08-26" passage="Real gross domestic product (GDP) increased at an annual rate of 1.5 percent" /><Cite s="bea-gdp-q2-second-estimate-2026-08-26" passage="Real final sales to private domestic purchasers" /></p>
checks/audits/fed-rate--2026-09-16-unanimous-quarter-point-hike-2026-09-20-since-8bf7b3a6-entailment.log:5099:+        <p data-layer="narrative">Headline economic growth slowed to {growth.secondQuarterGdp} percent from {growth.firstQuarterGdp} percent. Beneath that slowdown, consumer spending and private fixed investment grew {growth.secondQuarterPrivateDomesticSales} percent, a measure of domestic demand.<Cite s="bea-gdp-q2-second-estimate-2026-08-26" passage="Real gross domestic product (GDP) increased at an annual rate of 1.5 percent" /><Cite s="bea-gdp-q2-second-estimate-2026-08-26" passage="the sum of consumer spending and gross private fixed investment, increased 4.2 percent" /></p>
