y-root"><p class="story-kicker" data-layer="narrative"><a href="/events/fed-rate/">The federal funds rate</a> &middot; September 16, 2026</p><h1 data-layer="fact">Fed raises rates a quarter point on a unanimous vote</h1><p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">The quarter-point increase cannot lower oil prices. It targets demand and the spread of the energy shock, with the quickest effects reaching variable-rate borrowers and savers.</p><div class="mode-switcher" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Reading mode"><a class="mode-btn active" href="?mode=story" data-mode="story" role="radio" aria-checked="true">The story</a><a class="mode-btn" href="?mode=facts" data-mode="facts" role="radio" aria-checked="false">Just the facts</a><a class="mode-btn" href="?mode=work" data-mode="work" role="radio" aria-checked="false">Show the work</a></div><script type="module">document.addEventListener(`DOMContentLoaded`,()=>{let e=document.getElementById(`story-root`),t=document.querySelectorAll(`.mode-btn`);if(!e||!t.length)return;let n=new URLSearchParams(window.location.search),r=[`story`,`facts`,`work`].includes(n.get(`mode`))?n.get(`mode`):`story`;e.classList.add(`mode-${r}`);let i=e.querySelector(`[data-record-ledger]`);i&&(i.open=r===`work`),t.forEach(e=>{let t=e.getAttribute(`data-mode`)===r;e.classList.toggle(`active`,t),e.setAttribute(`aria-checked`,t?`true`:`false`),e.setAttribute(`tabindex`,t?`0`:`-1`)}),t.forEach((n,r)=>{n.addEventListener(`keydown`,e=>{let n=e.key===`ArrowRight`||e.key===`ArrowDown`?1:e.key===`ArrowLeft`||e.key===`ArrowUp`?-1:0;if(!n&&e.key!==`Home`&&e.key!==`End`&&e.key!==` `)return;e.preventDefault();let i=e.key===`Home`?t[0]:e.key===`End`?t[t.length-1]:t[(r+n+t.length)%t.length];i.click(),i.focus()}),n.addEventListener(`click`,r=>{r.preventDefault();let a=[...e.querySelectorAll(`section[id], aside[id]`)],o=a.find(e=>`#`+e.id===location.hash&&e.getBoundingClientRect().top>=0&&e.getBoundingClientRect().top<innerHeight)??a.filter(e=>e.getBoundingClientRect().height&&e.getBoundingClientRect().top<=160).at(-1),s=n.getAttribute(`data-mode`);i&&(i.open=s===`work`),e.className=e.className.replace(/mode-\w+/g,``).trim()+` mode-${s}`,t.forEach(e=>{let t=e===n;e.classList.toggle(`active`,t),e.setAttribute(`aria-checked`,t?`true`:`false`),e.setAttribute(`tabindex`,t?`0`:`-1`)});let c=new URL(window.location.href);if(c.searchParams.set(`mode`,s),o){let e=a.indexOf(o),t=o.getBoundingClientRect().height?o:[...a.slice(0,e).reverse(),...a.slice(e+1)].find(e=>e.getBoundingClientRect().height);t&&(requestAnimationFrame(()=>t.scrollIntoView({block:`start`})),c.hash=t.id)}history.replaceState(null,``,c.toString()),document.dispatchEvent(new Event(`reading-mode-change`))})})});</script><nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact"><details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><ol><li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li><li><a href="#what-rates-can-change">What a rate hike can change</a></li><li><a href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a></li><li><a href="#projections">The projections</a></li><li><a href="#the-catch">The catch</a></li><li><a href="#political-pressure">The pressure around the decision</a></li><li><a href="#what-happened-next">What happened next</a></li><li><a href="#unknowns">What we do not know yet</a></li><li><a href="#records">The records</a></li></ol></details></nav><div class="kpi-strip"><div class="kpi"><span class="kpi-value">3.75–4.00<small> %</small></span><span class="kpi-label">new target range</span></div><div class="kpi"><span class="kpi-value">12–0<small></small></span><span class="kpi-label">committee vote</span></div><div class="kpi"><span class="kpi-value">3.9<small> %</small></span><span class="kpi-label">August gasoline increase</span></div><div class="kpi"><span class="kpi-value">4.1<small> %</small></span><span class="kpi-label">median 2026 rate projection</span></div></div><p class="railed rail-note" data-layer="fact">Story updated 2026-09-19.</p><div data-layer="proof" class="event-record-context"><p class="sources-line">This story rests on the September policy statement, implementation note, projections and press-conference transcript; the July statement and minutes; the Federal Reserve's policy framework and Jackson Hole speech; August CPI and second-quarter GDP releases; fixed rate-series files; and coverage checked against those records. The press-conference transcript is marked preliminary on every page. The Associated Press reconstruction was retained by the capture registry as extracted text and markdown, but its run did not retain a raw-body receipt. Its two attributed observations are used with that limitation recorded in the manifest and working note.</p></div><section id="what-happened"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The decision</p><h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2><div class="sourced-block" data-layer="fact" data-source="fomc-statement-2026-09-16"><p>The Federal Open Market Committee voted 12–0 to raise its target range by one-quarter percentage point, to 3.75–4.00 percent.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/fomc-statement-2026-09-16/" data-passage="approved the following statement for release by a 12 – 0 vote" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="raise the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point">1</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><p data-layer="narrative">The new range took effect September 17. The Fed also raised the rate paid on reserve balances to 3.90 percent and the primary-credit rate to 4 percent.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-2" data-record-href="/records/fomc-implementation-2026-09-16/" data-passage="effective September 17, 2026" aria-label="Source 2: read the evidence" data-passages="increase in the primary credit rate to 4.0 percent">2</a></sup></p><figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact"><figcaption><strong>The first increase since 2023</strong><span>Upper limit of the target range</span></figcaption><figure class="rate-chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 720 224" role="img" aria-label="Federal funds target range upper limit from January 2024 through September 19, 2026"><g><line x1="48" y1="192" x2="712" y2="192" stroke="#e9ebee" stroke-width="1"></line><text x="42" y="195.5" text-anchor="end" class="chart-axis">3.50%</text></g><g><line x1="48" y1="151.11111111111111" x2="712" y2="151.11111111111111" stroke="#e9ebee" stroke-width="1"></line><text x="42" y="154.61111111111111" text-anchor="end" class="chart-axis">4%</text></g><g><line x1="48" y1="110.22222222222223" x2="712" y2="110.22222222222223" stroke="#e9ebee" stroke-width="1"></line><text x="42" y="113.72222222222223" text-anchor="end" class="chart-axis">4.50%</text></g><g><line x1="48" y1="69.33333333333334" x2="712" y2="69.33333333333334" stroke="#e9ebee" stroke-width="1"></line><text x="42" y="72.83333333333334" text-anchor="end" class="chart-axis">5%</text></g><g><line x1="48" y1="28.444444444444457" x2="712" y2="28.444444444444457" stroke="#e9ebee" stroke-width="1"></line><text x="42" y="31.944444444444457" text-anchor="end" class="chart-axis">5.50%</text></g><path d="M48,28.444444444444457 L223.3709677419355,28.444444444444457 L223.3709677419355,69.33333333333334 L256.8387096774194,69.33333333333334 L256.8387096774194,89.77777777777777 L284.2822580645161,89.77777777777777 L284.2822580645161,110.22222222222223 L467.0161290322581,110.22222222222223 L467.0161290322581,130.66666666666669 L495.1290322580645,130.66666666666669 L495.1290322580645,151.11111111111111 L523.241935483871,151.11111111111111 L523.241935483871,171.55555555555554 L710.6612903225806,171.55555555555554 L710.6612903225806,151.11111111111111 L712,151.11111111111111" fill="none" stroke="#7a9cc4" stroke-width="2.5"></path><circle cx="712" cy="151.11111111111111" r="4.5" fill="#c98a1b"></circle><text x="48" y="20.444444444444457" text-anchor="start" class="chart-note">5.50%</text><text x="712" y="143.11111111111111" text-anchor="end" class="chart-note chart-note-accent">4.00%</text><text x="-193.63709677419354" y="216" class="chart-axis">2023</text><text x="50.67741935483871" y="216" class="chart-axis">2024</text><text x="295.6612903225806" y="216" class="chart-axis">2025</text><text x="539.9758064516129" y="216" class="chart-axis">2026</text></svg><figcaption>The upper bound fell in six steps from September 2024 through December 2025, then rose a quarter point effective September 17, 2026.</figcaption></figure><p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">Computed from 6,487 daily DFEDTARU observations saved September 19.</p></figure><p data-layer="narrative">Seven weeks earlier, the committee had held the range at 3.50–3.75 percent by a 9–3 vote. The July minutes say most participants wanted more information before acting, while several already favored a quarter-point increase.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-6" data-record-href="/records/fomc-statement-2026-07-29/" data-passage="approved the following statement for release by a 9 – 3 vote" aria-label="Source 6: read the evidence">6</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-22" data-record-href="/records/fomc-minutes-2026-07-29/" data-passage="most participants supported maintaining the current target range" aria-label="Source 22: read the evidence" data-passages="Several participants favored an increase of 25 basis points">22</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">By late August, Chair Kevin Warsh had set a public test: underlying inflation had to move toward the Fed's goal “clearly and at sufficient speed.” The next official CPI release showed gasoline up 3.9 percent in August, accounting for more than one-third of the monthly increase, while core CPI was 2.4 percent over the year.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-23" data-record-href="/records/fed-warsh-jackson-hole-2026-08-28/" data-passage="clearly and at sufficient speed" aria-label="Source 23: read the evidence">23</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-20" data-record-href="/records/bls-cpi-2026-09-11/" data-passage="gasoline rose 3.9 percent in August, accounting for over one third" aria-label="Source 20: read the evidence" data-passages="all items less food and energy index rose 2.4 percent over the year">20</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">Growth was not uniformly fast: real GDP increased at a 1.5 percent annual rate in the second quarter, down from 2.1 percent in the first. Private domestic final sales, consumer spending plus private fixed investment, grew 4.2 percent in the same quarter.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-21" data-record-href="/records/bea-gdp-q2-second-estimate-2026-08-26/" data-passage="Real gross domestic product (GDP) increased at an annual rate of 1.5 percent" aria-label="Source 21: read the evidence" data-passages="Real final sales to private domestic purchasers">21</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">Warsh highlighted three considerations in explaining the September decision: the economy had strengthened, his judgment that summer inflation trends had not passed the test remained, and the committee's geopolitical assessment had changed. The September statement described resilient domestic spending, robust capital investment and elevated inflation. Warsh described the increase as removing “a dose of accommodation” so financial and credit conditions would be more consistent with the Fed's objectives.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-5" data-record-href="/records/fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16/" data-passage="I'll highlight three things that have happened in that intermeeting period" aria-label="Source 5: read the evidence">5</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/fomc-statement-2026-09-16/" data-passage="domestic spending has been resilient" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-5" data-record-href="/records/fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16/" data-passage="We removed a dose of accommodation" aria-label="Source 5: read the evidence">5</a></sup></p><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="fomc-statement-2026-09-16"><figure class="data-table-wrap"><table class="data-table"><caption>Selected statement lines, read directly from the two saved releases</caption><thead><tr><th class>Line</th><th class>July 29</th><th class>September 16</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class>Vote</td><td class>The Federal Open Market Committee approved the following statement for release by a 9 – 3 vote:</td><td class>The Federal Open Market Committee approved the following statement for release by a 12 – 0 vote:</td></tr><tr><td class>Rate decision</td><td class>The Committee decided to maintain the target range for the federal funds rate at 3-1/2 to 3-3/4 percent, in support of the Federal Reserve&#39;s dual mandate. The Committee is continuing its policy of maintaining ample reserves in the banking system.</td><td class>The Committee decided to raise the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point to 3-3/4 to 4 percent, in support of the Federal Reserve&#39;s dual mandate. The Committee is continuing its policy of maintaining ample reserves in the banking system.</td></tr><tr><td class>Economy</td><td class>Economic activity is expanding at a solid pace despite elevated uncertainty that owes, in part, to the conflict in the Middle East. Productivity growth and capital investment are strong. Job gains have kept pace with the workforce, and the unemployment rate has changed little.</td><td class>Economic activity is expanding at a solid pace. While uncertainty remains elevated owing, in part, to geopolitical developments, domestic spending has been resilient. Productivity growth is strong, and capital investment is robust. Job gains have kept pace with the workforce, and the unemployment rate has changed little.</td></tr><tr><td class>Inflation</td><td class>Inflation remains elevated relative to the Committee&#39;s 2 percent goal, in part reflecting supply shocks that have driven price increases in certain sectors, including energy. The Committee will deliver price stability.</td><td class>Inflation remains elevated. Today&#39;s policy action will support a timelier return to the Committee&#39;s 2 percent goal. The Committee will deliver price stability.</td></tr><tr><td class>Dissents</td><td class>Voting against the monetary policy action were Beth M. Hammack, Neel Kashkari, and Lorie K. Logan, who preferred to raise the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point at this meeting.</td><td class>Not present</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div></section><section id="what-rates-can-change"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The mechanism</p><h2 data-layer="fact">What a rate hike can change</h2><p data-layer="narrative">The Fed raises the cost of borrowing to slow consumer and business spending, reducing demand.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-17" data-record-href="/records/pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16/" data-passage="slow consumer and business spending by raising the cost of borrowing" aria-label="Source 17: read the evidence">17</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">The Associated Press traced the oil and gasoline shock to the war with Iran, which was in its seventh month by the day after the decision.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-24" data-record-href="/records/ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17/" data-passage="with the Iran war now in its seventh month" aria-label="Source 24: read the evidence">24</a></sup></p><div class="sourced-block" data-layer="fact" data-source="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16"><p>A rate increase cannot set the price of oil or gasoline. Warsh said the Fed cannot affect an individual price; it can try to stop a relative-price shock from broadening into second- and third-order effects across the economy.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-5" data-record-href="/records/fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16/" data-passage="We cannot affect any individual price" aria-label="Source 5: read the evidence" data-passages="Don't have second and third order effects in the economy">5</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><p data-layer="narrative">The August CPI increase was partly an energy-price event: gasoline supplied more than a third of the monthly rise. Warsh framed the Fed's task as preventing that relative-price shock from producing second- and third-order effects across the economy.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-20" data-record-href="/records/bls-cpi-2026-09-11/" data-passage="gasoline rose 3.9 percent in August, accounting for over one third" aria-label="Source 20: read the evidence">20</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-5" data-record-href="/records/fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16/" data-passage="Don't have second and third order effects in the economy" aria-label="Source 5: read the evidence">5</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">The effect is delayed. The Fed's strategy statement says monetary policy influences activity, employment and prices with a lag, which creates a risk that an energy shock fades before tighter credit has fully slowed the economy.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-8" data-record-href="/records/fomc-longer-run-goals-2025/" data-passage="Monetary policy actions tend to influence economic activity, employment, and prices with a lag" aria-label="Source 8: read the evidence">8</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-24" data-record-href="/records/ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17/" data-passage="By the time higher borrowing costs started to slow the economy, the gas price shock could be over" aria-label="Source 24: read the evidence">24</a></sup></p></section><section id="who-feels-it"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Borrowers and savers</p><h2 data-layer="fact">Who feels it</h2><div class="sourced-block" data-layer="fact" data-source="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16"><p>Credit-card rates can respond quickly because most cards carry variable rates tied to banks' prime rate. Auto loans and adjustable-rate mortgages can also become more expensive, while savings accounts and certificates of deposit may pay more.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-17" data-record-href="/records/pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16/" data-passage="Fed's policy changes flow through to credit card rates rapidly" aria-label="Source 17: read the evidence" data-passages="interest rates on savings accounts and certificates of deposit are headed higher">17</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-checked">checked</span></div><p data-layer="narrative">Fixed mortgage rates are different: they tend to follow the 10-year Treasury yield, not the Fed's rate directly. Homeowners with fixed loans do not have their existing rate reset by this decision.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-17" data-record-href="/records/pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16/" data-passage="Mortgage rates don't necessarily follow what the Fed does" aria-label="Source 17: read the evidence" data-passages="many homeowners locked in low mortgage rates">17</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">Consumer-finance analyst Matt Schulz told the Associated Press that one quarter-point increase would not have a large effect. He said the larger burden would come if several increases stack up over time.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-17" data-record-href="/records/pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16/" data-passage="single quarter-point rate increase isn't really going to have a huge impact" aria-label="Source 17: read the evidence" data-passages="stack a few these on top of each other over time">17</a></sup></p></section><section id="projections"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The signal beyond the vote</p><h2 data-layer="fact">The projections</h2><p data-layer="narrative">The median projected federal funds rate for the end of 2026 rose from 3.8 percent in June to 4.1 percent in September. For 2027, the median rose from 3.6 percent in June to 4.1 percent in September.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-4" data-record-href="/records/fomc-sep-2026-09-16/" data-passage="Federal funds rate         4.1    4.1" aria-label="Source 4: read the evidence" data-passages="June projection          3.8    3.6">4</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">The distribution shows several paths around the median: 12 of 18 officials projected a single hike, 4 projected two hikes, and 2 predicted a pair of holds. Warsh did not submit a projection of his own.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-16" data-record-href="/records/the-hill-fed-decision-2026-09-16/" data-passage="Twelve of 18 FOMC officials projected a single rate hike" aria-label="Source 16: read the evidence" data-passages="Four officials projected two hikes, while two predicted a pair of holds">16</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-5" data-record-href="/records/fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16/" data-passage="I have not offered a projection of" aria-label="Source 5: read the evidence">5</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">The projection document defines the dots as participants' individual assessments of appropriate monetary policy. Warsh said his practice is not to give forward guidance or prejudge future actions.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-4" data-record-href="/records/fomc-sep-2026-09-16/" data-passage="projections of participants’ individual assessments of appropriate monetary policy" aria-label="Source 4: read the evidence">4</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-5" data-record-href="/records/fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16/" data-passage="My business is to not give forward guidance" aria-label="Source 5: read the evidence">5</a></sup></p><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="fomc-sep-2026-09-16"><figure class="data-table-wrap"><table class="data-table"><caption>September participant medians in Table 1</caption><thead><tr><th class>Median projection, percent</th><th class>2026</th><th class>2027</th><th class>2028</th><th class>2029</th><th class>Longer run</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class>Real GDP growth</td><td class>2.3</td><td class>2.4</td><td class>2.2</td><td class>2.1</td><td class>2</td></tr><tr><td class>Unemployment rate</td><td class>4.1</td><td class>4.1</td><td class>4.1</td><td class>4.1</td><td class>4.2</td></tr><tr><td class>PCE inflation</td><td class>3.7</td><td class>2.3</td><td class>2.1</td><td class>2</td><td class>2</td></tr><tr><td class>Core PCE inflation</td><td class>3.4</td><td class>2.5</td><td class>2.2</td><td class>2</td><td class>not collected</td></tr><tr><td class>Federal funds rate</td><td class>4.1</td><td class>4.1</td><td class>3.9</td><td class>3.6</td><td class>3.2</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div></section><section id="the-catch"><aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact"><p class="tt-label">The catch</p><ol><li><h3>The 2027 median was 4.1 percent.</h3>CBS wrote that policymakers expected to hold rates steady throughout 2027. The record says something narrower: the median of individual projections was 4.1 percent in both years, about half of participants projected a steady rate next year, and the chair declined to give forward guidance.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-14" data-record-href="/records/cbs-fed-decision-2026-09-16/" data-passage="policymakers expect to hold rates steady throughout 2027" aria-label="Source 14: read the evidence" data-passages="About half of FOMC members predicted that rates would hold steady next year">14</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-4" data-record-href="/records/fomc-sep-2026-09-16/" data-passage="For each period, the median is the middle projection" aria-label="Source 4: read the evidence">4</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-5" data-record-href="/records/fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16/" data-passage="ill-prepared to pre-judge those future actions" aria-label="Source 5: read the evidence">5</a></sup></li><li><h3>One vote, eighteen projections.</h3>Twelve committee members voted on the rate increase. Eighteen participants submitted projections. The vote applied to the September action; the projection record contains several paths after it.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/fomc-statement-2026-09-16/" data-passage="12 – 0 vote" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-4" data-record-href="/records/fomc-sep-2026-09-16/" data-passage="Eighteen participants submitted information" aria-label="Source 4: read the evidence">4</a></sup></li></ol></aside></section><section id="political-pressure"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The White House and the central bank</p><h2 data-layer="fact">The pressure around the decision</h2><div class="sourced-block" data-layer="fact" data-source="the-hill-trump-fed-reaction-2026-09-16"><p>About two hours after the announcement, The Hill reported that President Donald Trump demanded rates of 1 percent or less and called for the Fed to lower them fast.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-18" data-record-href="/records/the-hill-trump-fed-reaction-2026-09-16/" data-passage="Interest Rates in the United States should be 1%, or less" aria-label="Source 18: read the evidence" data-passages="Trump’s reaction came about two hours">18</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-checked">checked</span></div><p data-layer="narrative">Warsh described central-bank independence as “a two-way street” and said trade and fiscal policymakers should stay in their lane.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-16" data-record-href="/records/the-hill-fed-decision-2026-09-16/" data-passage="is a two-way street" aria-label="Source 16: read the evidence">16</a></sup></p></section><section id="what-happened-next"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">September 17 and 18</p><h2 data-layer="fact">What happened next</h2><div class="sourced-block" data-layer="fact" data-source="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17"><p>The next day, the 10-year Treasury yield slipped. The Associated Press described that as a likely sign that investors' inflation worries had eased, while noting that mortgage rates and other long-term borrowing costs are not directly controlled by the Fed.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-24" data-record-href="/records/ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17/" data-passage="10-year Treasury yield even slipped a bit" aria-label="Source 24: read the evidence" data-passages="The Fed’s rate can influence longer-term costs such as mortgage rates, but the Fed doesn’t directly control them">24</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-checked">checked</span></div><p data-layer="narrative">By September 18, CNBC reported that market-implied odds of another increase in October had risen to about 58 percent from 42 percent a week earlier. Goldman Sachs expected an October increase, Bank of America expected increases in October and December, and one strategist said the move was not the start of an aggressive new tightening cycle.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-19" data-record-href="/records/cnbc-warsh-followup-2026-09-18/" data-passage="near 58% Friday morning" aria-label="Source 19: read the evidence" data-passages="We remain unconvinced that this is the start of an aggressive new tightening cycle">19</a></sup></p><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="fomc-calendar-2026-09-19"><figure class="data-table-wrap"><table class="data-table"><caption>Decision chronology</caption><thead><tr><th class>When</th><th class>Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class>July 29</td><td class>The committee holds at 3.50–3.75 percent in a 9–3 vote; three members prefer a quarter-point increase.</td></tr><tr><td class>September 16, 2 p.m.</td><td class>The committee raises the range to 3.75–4 percent in a 12–0 vote and releases new projections.</td></tr><tr><td class>September 16, 2:30 p.m.</td><td class>Chair Kevin Warsh holds a press conference and says the committee is not providing forward guidance.</td></tr><tr><td class>September 17</td><td class>The new target range, reserve-balance rate and primary-credit rate take effect.</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div></section><section id="unknowns"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions</p><h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2><ul data-layer="fact"><li>Whether another increase follows this one. The projections are individual assessments of appropriate monetary policy, and Warsh declined to prejudge the next decision.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-4" data-record-href="/records/fomc-sep-2026-09-16/" data-passage="individual assessments of appropriate monetary policy" aria-label="Source 4: read the evidence">4</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-5" data-record-href="/records/fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16/" data-passage="ill-prepared to pre-judge those future actions" aria-label="Source 5: read the evidence">5</a></sup></li><li>How long the energy shock lasts. Monetary policy works with a lag, and the Associated Press described the risk that the gas-price shock could be over before higher borrowing costs started to slow the economy.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-24" data-record-href="/records/ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17/" data-passage="the gas price shock could be over" aria-label="Source 24: read the evidence">24</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-8" data-record-href="/records/fomc-longer-run-goals-2025/" data-passage="influence economic activity, employment, and prices with a lag" aria-label="Source 8: read the evidence">8</a></sup></li><li>How much households ultimately pay. The Associated Press analysis says several increases would stack up over time, while fixed mortgage rates can move differently from the policy rate.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-17" data-record-href="/records/pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16/" data-passage="stack a few these on top of each other over time" aria-label="Source 17: read the evidence" data-passages="Mortgage rates don't necessarily follow what the Fed does">17</a></sup></li><li>What the September deliberations add. The official calendar says regular-meeting minutes are released three weeks after the decision; its September minutes slot was blank in the September 19 capture.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-7" data-record-href="/records/fomc-calendar-2026-09-19/" data-passage="The minutes of regularly scheduled meetings are released three weeks after the date of the policy decision." aria-label="Source 7: read the evidence">7</a></sup></li></ul></section><section id="records" data-layer="fact"><p class="sec-kicker">Sources</p><h2>The records</h2><p class="section-lede">Everything this page rests on, numbered where the story cites it. We keep a dated copy of each one.</p><h3 class="src-group">Primary documents</h3><ol class="src-list" start="1"><li id="src-1" value="1"><a href="/records/fomc-statement-2026-09-16/">September 16 FOMC statement</a> · Federal Reserve Board, 2026-09-16 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the target range, vote and statement comparison</span></li><li id="src-2" value="2"><a href="/records/fomc-implementation-2026-09-16/">September 16 implementation note</a> · Federal Reserve Board, 2026-09-16 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the effective date and operating rates</span></li><li id="src-3" value="3"><a href="/records/fomc-sep-release-2026-09-16/">September projections release</a> · Federal Reserve Board, 2026-09-16 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the release record for the projections</span></li><li id="src-4" value="4"><a href="/records/fomc-sep-2026-09-16/">Summary of Economic Projections</a> · Federal Reserve Board, 2026-09-16 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; Table 1 medians and participant distributions</span></li><li id="src-5" value="5"><a href="/records/fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16/">Chair Warsh press conference transcript</a> · Federal Reserve Board, 2026-09-16 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; Warsh&#39;s description of the decision and projections</span></li><li id="src-6" value="6"><a href="/records/fomc-statement-2026-07-29/">July 29 FOMC statement</a> · Federal Reserve Board, 2026-07-29 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the previous statement in the redline</span></li><li id="src-7" value="7"><a href="/records/fomc-calendar-2026-09-19/">FOMC meeting calendar</a> · Federal Reserve Board, captured 2026-09-19 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the meeting record and unavailable minutes</span></li><li id="src-8" value="8"><a href="/records/fomc-longer-run-goals-2025/">Longer-run goals and policy strategy</a> · Federal Reserve Board, 2025-08-22 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the mandate and policy-tool framework</span></li><li id="src-22" value="22"><a href="/records/fomc-minutes-2026-07-29/">July 28–29 FOMC minutes</a> · Federal Reserve Board, released 2026-08-19 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the prior preference to wait and the case for an increase</span></li><li id="src-23" value="23"><a href="/records/fed-warsh-jackson-hole-2026-08-28/">Warsh&#39;s Jackson Hole remarks</a> · Federal Reserve Board, 2026-08-28 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the inflation standard stated before the meeting</span></li></ol><h3 class="src-group">Official data</h3><ol class="src-list" start="9"><li id="src-9" value="9"><a href="/records/fred-dfedtaru-2026-09-19/">DFEDTARU daily series</a> · Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis, through 2026-09-19 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the target-range path and derived figures</span></li><li id="src-10" value="10"><a href="/records/fred-dfedtar-2026-09-19/">DFEDTA